1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,086
If you can't pass this test,

2
00:00:02,086 --> 00:00:06,168
you're likely to suffer big losses
in stocks, just like Mr. Kim.

3
00:00:06,548 --> 00:00:10,708
In a little over 3 years, the
S&P 500 has risen more than 70%.

4
00:00:11,088 --> 00:00:15,030
Over the same period, Mr. Kim instead
lost 74% of his 100 million won,

5
00:00:15,030 --> 00:00:16,888
and only 26 million won was left.

6
00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:21,180
So what on earth happened to Mr.
Kim, and what choices did he make?

7
00:00:21,560 --> 00:00:26,260
At the five crossroads Mr. Kim
faced, choose what you would do.

8
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,381
Just count how many you get wrong.

9
00:00:28,761 --> 00:00:30,985
From now on, you are Mr. Kim.

10
00:00:31,985 --> 00:00:34,513
You have 100 million won in savings.

11
00:00:34,893 --> 00:00:37,999
Everyone's talking about a US tech
stock that went up 3x in a year.

12
00:00:38,379 --> 00:00:40,868
It's a company that uses
AI to approve loans,

13
00:00:40,868 --> 00:00:43,136
and it's up more than 5x this year alone.

14
00:00:43,516 --> 00:00:47,415
On the day a big investment firm agreed
to buy a large batch of its loans,

15
00:00:47,415 --> 00:00:48,996
it jumped 35% in a single day.

16
00:00:49,376 --> 00:00:51,454
Your coworkers and friends
say they bought it,

17
00:00:51,454 --> 00:00:53,216
and they're bragging about their gains.

18
00:00:53,596 --> 00:00:57,068
YouTube is flooded with videos
saying it will go even higher.

19
00:00:57,448 --> 00:01:01,062
So, do you buy this tech
stock like everyone else?

20
00:01:01,442 --> 00:01:03,966
Or do you skip buying this stock?

21
00:01:04,346 --> 00:01:05,286
Make your choice.

22
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:07,846
3, 2, 1.

23
00:01:08,226 --> 00:01:10,702
Here, not buying is the right choice.

24
00:01:11,082 --> 00:01:15,212
The date was August 1, 2023,
and the stock was Upstart.

25
00:01:15,592 --> 00:01:18,642
If you bought like everyone
else, as Mr. Kim did,

26
00:01:18,642 --> 00:01:22,252
it fell as much as 71%, and
you'd still be down 67% today.

27
00:01:22,632 --> 00:01:26,172
Of course, when everyone says it's
good, it seems there must be a reason.

28
00:01:26,552 --> 00:01:30,822
But by the time everyone is praising
it, it has often already risen a lot.

29
00:01:31,202 --> 00:01:33,522
This stock had already
gone up 3x in a year,

30
00:01:33,522 --> 00:01:36,844
so buying it also meant buying
the risk of an equally big fall.

31
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:40,059
So the right choice is not to
buy just because others do.

32
00:01:41,059 --> 00:01:44,065
On the same day, you have
one more decision to make.

33
00:01:44,445 --> 00:01:46,502
How much of the 100
million won do you put in?

34
00:01:46,882 --> 00:01:49,417
Many people around you say
holding cash is a loss,

35
00:01:49,417 --> 00:01:51,648
and that they put in
all the money they had.

36
00:01:52,028 --> 00:01:56,452
So, do you keep some cash aside for
emergencies and invest only the rest?

37
00:01:56,832 --> 00:01:59,105
Or do you invest all 100 million won?

38
00:01:59,485 --> 00:02:00,425
Make your choice.

39
00:02:00,805 --> 00:02:02,985
3, 2, 1.

40
00:02:03,365 --> 00:02:07,208
Here, keeping cash aside for
emergencies is the right choice.

41
00:02:07,588 --> 00:02:13,607
That day was August 1, 2023, and in less
than three months the S&P 500 fell 10%.

42
00:02:13,987 --> 00:02:16,403
If you'd invested it all, like Mr. Kim,

43
00:02:16,403 --> 00:02:19,253
you'd have lost nearly
10%, even in the index.

44
00:02:19,633 --> 00:02:21,530
If you'd suddenly needed a large sum then,

45
00:02:21,530 --> 00:02:23,970
you'd have had to sell your
stocks at the lower price.

46
00:02:24,350 --> 00:02:28,299
Of course, money kept in cash earns
only interest while the market rises.

47
00:02:28,679 --> 00:02:31,879
But worst cases, like losing
your job or a housing problem,

48
00:02:31,879 --> 00:02:34,049
often come right when the market is bad.

49
00:02:34,429 --> 00:02:38,288
With cash set aside, you can hold on
without selling your stocks, even then.

50
00:02:38,668 --> 00:02:42,803
So the right choice is to keep cash
aside to prepare for the worst.

51
00:02:43,803 --> 00:02:45,577
About a year and a half later,

52
00:02:45,577 --> 00:02:49,422
tariff fears pushed the S&P 500
down more than 10% from its peak.

53
00:02:49,802 --> 00:02:53,582
The news is split between "it will
fall further" and "this is the bottom."

54
00:02:53,962 --> 00:02:56,297
Forums are full of screenshots of losses.

55
00:02:56,677 --> 00:02:58,868
So, do you decide whether to buy or sell

56
00:02:58,868 --> 00:03:01,333
based on the news and
the mood of the moment?

57
00:03:01,713 --> 00:03:03,880
Or do you cut your position by a set rule,

58
00:03:03,880 --> 00:03:07,027
and buy back in parts each time
it falls by a set percentage?

59
00:03:07,407 --> 00:03:08,347
Make your choice.

60
00:03:08,727 --> 00:03:10,907
3, 2, 1.

61
00:03:11,287 --> 00:03:13,697
Here, cutting your
position and buying back

62
00:03:13,697 --> 00:03:16,164
in parts with each drop
is the right choice.

63
00:03:16,544 --> 00:03:22,234
That day was March 13, 2025, when
the S&P 500 was 10% below its peak.

64
00:03:22,614 --> 00:03:23,992
What if, like Mr. Kim,

65
00:03:23,992 --> 00:03:27,940
you went by the mood and sold
everything at the low on April 8?

66
00:03:28,320 --> 00:03:32,352
If you only bought back after it
regained its high 80 days later,

67
00:03:32,352 --> 00:03:34,151
you'd be up just 16% to date.

68
00:03:34,531 --> 00:03:37,066
The numbers in a rule can
differ from person to person.

69
00:03:37,446 --> 00:03:40,132
For example, say you
sell half that day and

70
00:03:40,132 --> 00:03:42,880
buy back 1/3 every time
it falls another 5%.

71
00:03:43,260 --> 00:03:46,183
Then on April 4, as it
drops 5% and then 10%

72
00:03:46,183 --> 00:03:49,040
below your selling
price, you buy back 2/3.

73
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:53,090
The remaining 1/3 you buy on the
day the index regains its high.

74
00:03:53,470 --> 00:03:58,092
Following this rule, you'd be
up 46% from that day to now.

75
00:03:58,472 --> 00:04:02,820
Of course, even with a rule, you can't
know in advance where a fall will stop.

76
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:05,300
But if you set your rule in advance,

77
00:04:05,300 --> 00:04:08,159
you can make the same call
even in scary moments.

78
00:04:08,539 --> 00:04:11,826
So the right choice is to cut
your position when it's risky,

79
00:04:11,826 --> 00:04:13,689
and buy back in parts as it falls.

80
00:04:14,689 --> 00:04:17,610
About three months later,
on an acquaintance's tip,

81
00:04:17,610 --> 00:04:20,072
you put half your
account into an AI stock.

82
00:04:20,452 --> 00:04:21,821
A little over a month later,

83
00:04:21,821 --> 00:04:23,824
the company reported far lower sales than

84
00:04:23,824 --> 00:04:25,975
expected and withdrew
its earnings forecast.

85
00:04:26,355 --> 00:04:29,827
Something went wrong with the company's
basic health, its fundamentals.

86
00:04:30,207 --> 00:04:33,172
The stock fell more than
25% in a single day.

87
00:04:33,552 --> 00:04:37,855
So, since the company's fundamentals
are broken, do you sell right away?

88
00:04:38,235 --> 00:04:40,928
Or do you hold on until
you get your money back?

89
00:04:41,308 --> 00:04:42,248
Make your choice.

90
00:04:42,628 --> 00:04:44,808
3, 2, 1.

91
00:04:45,188 --> 00:04:47,402
Here, selling on the
very day the company's

92
00:04:47,402 --> 00:04:49,308
problem appeared is the right choice.

93
00:04:49,688 --> 00:04:53,544
That day was August 11, 2025,
and the stock was C3.ai,

94
00:04:53,544 --> 00:04:56,258
which sells AI software to businesses.

95
00:04:56,638 --> 00:05:00,076
If you held on like Mr.
Kim, it fell another 53%,

96
00:05:00,076 --> 00:05:03,022
and it's still 25% below that day's price.

97
00:05:03,402 --> 00:05:08,138
Had you sold right away, you'd
have avoided that entire 53% drop.

98
00:05:08,518 --> 00:05:11,562
Of course, selling after
a drop of more than 25% in

99
00:05:11,562 --> 00:05:14,427
a day locks in the loss,
and that feels painful.

100
00:05:14,807 --> 00:05:16,865
But the price you paid has nothing to do

101
00:05:16,865 --> 00:05:19,077
with whether the stock
will rise from here.

102
00:05:19,457 --> 00:05:24,047
If the earnings behind the price collapse,
holding on longer can deepen the loss.

103
00:05:24,427 --> 00:05:27,797
One study looked at the trades of
10,000 individual investors in the US.

104
00:05:28,177 --> 00:05:31,150
The winners they sold went
on to beat the losers they

105
00:05:31,150 --> 00:05:34,347
kept by more than 3 percentage
points over the next year.

106
00:05:34,727 --> 00:05:38,693
If you admit the mistake and sell, you can
start again with the money you have left.

107
00:05:39,073 --> 00:05:41,482
So the right choice is
to cut your losses right

108
00:05:41,482 --> 00:05:43,583
away when a company's fundamentals break.

109
00:05:44,583 --> 00:05:48,903
Two months later, quantum computing stocks
are hitting all-time highs day after day.

110
00:05:49,283 --> 00:05:52,792
One of them is up more than 7x in a year.

111
00:05:53,172 --> 00:05:55,290
Online communities are
overflowing with posts

112
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:57,502
from people who put half
their account into it.

113
00:05:57,882 --> 00:06:01,273
So, do you put half your
account into this stock?

114
00:06:01,653 --> 00:06:04,610
Or do you limit any one
stock to 10% of your account,

115
00:06:04,610 --> 00:06:07,120
and spread the rest
across different sectors?

116
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:08,440
Make your choice.

117
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,000
3, 2, 1.

118
00:06:11,380 --> 00:06:13,575
Here, capping one stock at 10% and

119
00:06:13,575 --> 00:06:16,352
spreading out the rest
is the right choice.

120
00:06:16,732 --> 00:06:18,785
That day was October 13, 2025,

121
00:06:18,785 --> 00:06:22,548
and the stock was IonQ, which
builds quantum computers.

122
00:06:22,928 --> 00:06:27,604
If you put in half like Mr. Kim, your
account dropped 36% at one point,

123
00:06:27,604 --> 00:06:29,843
and you'd still be down 21% today.

124
00:06:30,223 --> 00:06:33,373
With only 10% in it, your
account would have dropped

125
00:06:33,373 --> 00:06:36,220
just 11% at its worst,
and you'd be up 10% now.

126
00:06:36,600 --> 00:06:39,940
Of course, the more you put in, the
more you make when the stock rises.

127
00:06:40,320 --> 00:06:44,150
But if that one bet is wrong, half
your account goes down with it.

128
00:06:44,530 --> 00:06:46,773
If you set a limit for
each stock in advance,

129
00:06:46,773 --> 00:06:49,116
your account can survive
even if one collapses.

130
00:06:49,496 --> 00:06:52,092
So the right choice is
to set a limit per stock,

131
00:06:52,092 --> 00:06:54,958
and spread your money across
many stocks and sectors.

132
00:06:55,958 --> 00:06:58,459
So, how many did you
get wrong on this test?

133
00:06:58,839 --> 00:07:03,488
0 wrong: you invest by your own rules,
not by what others say. You pass.

134
00:07:03,868 --> 00:07:05,854
1 or 2 wrong: you know better,

135
00:07:05,854 --> 00:07:09,494
but others' talk and greed
sometimes sway you. Caution.

136
00:07:09,874 --> 00:07:15,684
3 or more wrong: like Mr. Kim, you're
likely to lose money in stocks. You fail.

137
00:07:16,684 --> 00:07:20,210
As this test shows, remember
that haste makes waste:

138
00:07:20,210 --> 00:07:23,262
letting go of greed and
going slow and steady

139
00:07:23,262 --> 00:07:25,974
is the only way to get rich with stocks.

140
00:07:26,354 --> 00:07:28,564
Wishing you all successful investing.

141
00:07:28,944 --> 00:07:30,474
Thanks for watching.
